<Marq n="08" t="Conclusion" dir={-1} />

<!-- CONCLUSION -->
<section class="chapter conc" id="conclusion" data-scene="nodes" data-chapter="Conclusion" data-part="8">
  <div class="stack-v" style="max-width:980px">
    <p class="eyebrow">In conclusion</p>
    <h2 class="h-display" data-split>Pick the right tool <em>for the job.</em></h2>
    <p class="body-copy">There is no perfect database, relational or NoSQL. Every one has advantages and disadvantages that depend on the task. NoSQL is a set of concepts, ideas, technologies and software for big data, sparse semi-structured data, horizontal scale and massively parallel processing, and different applications need different NoSQL solutions.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scales">
    <article class="scale-pan rdb">
      <span class="tag sql">RDBMS</span>
      <h3>When data must be <em>right</em></h3>
      <ul><li>Solving ACID problems</li><li>Data validity is super important</li><li>You need dynamic, ad hoc queries and reports</li></ul>
    </article>
    <div class="scale-beam" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></div>
    <article class="scale-pan nos">
      <span class="tag kv">NoSQL</span>
      <h3>When data must be <em>available</em></h3>
      <ul><li>Solving data availability problems</li><li>Fast data matters more than perfectly current data</li><li>You must scale as requirements change</li></ul>
    </article>
  </div>
  <div class="dba">
    <div class="dba-pager" aria-hidden="true"><span>02:00</span><b>node-3 out of sync</b><em>checksum mismatch on shard B</em></div>
    <div class="stack-v">
      <p class="eyebrow">Don’t forget the DBA</p>
      <h3 class="h-sub">Who you gonna <em>call?</em></h3>
      <p class="body-copy">Moving data to NoSQL does not remove the operations work. Someone still owns <strong>backups and recovery</strong>, <strong>capacity planning</strong>, <strong>performance monitoring</strong>, <strong>data integration</strong> and <strong>tuning</strong>. When nodes fall out of sync or data corrupts at 2 a.m., the DBA and the system administrator need to be on board.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="summary-quote">
    <p>“Not every problem is a nail, and not every solution is a hammer.”</p>
    <span>From the module summary. Most data is still stored in relational databases; the biggest NoSQL users are social networks such as Twitter, Facebook and LinkedIn. And NoSQL brought database development, a field some had called dead, back to life.</span>
  </div>
</section>
    <!-- 19 RECAP -->
    <section class="chapter recap" id="recap" data-scene="nebula" data-chapter="Recap and quiz" data-part="8">
      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px;max-width:900px">
        <p class="eyebrow">Recap</p>
        <h2 class="h-display" data-split>Seven things to <em>keep.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="recap-rows">
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">01</span><h3>Distributed DBs</h3><p>Logically related databases at several sites, managed by a DDBMS that makes distribution transparent. Homogeneous or heterogeneous, federated or multidatabase; shared-nothing hardware; global and local schemas.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">02</span><h3>Fragments and copies</h3><p>Horizontal fragments by selection, vertical by projection (keep the key), or mixed; check completeness, reconstruction and disjointness. Allocate with no, partial or full replication.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">03</span><h3>Transactions and queries</h3><p>Two-phase commit: prepare, then commit or abort; it blocks if the coordinator fails. Locks via primary site, primary copy or voting. Query plans minimise data transfer; semijoins ship only join columns.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">04</span><h3>Why NoSQL</h3><p>Big, fast, sparse, semi-structured internet data and the need to scale out. Traits: horizontal scaling, replication, sharding, hashing or range access, no fixed schema, simple CRUD APIs, versioning.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">05</span><h3>CAP, ACID, BASE</h3><p>Under a partition, choose consistency or availability. ACID is strict and pessimistic; BASE is basically available, soft state, eventually consistent. Quorums with R + W &gt; N tune the balance.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">06</span><h3>Modelling and MapReduce</h3><p>Aggregates are the unit of storage and consistency; embed or reference. Distribute by sharding, master-slave or peer-to-peer replication. MapReduce splits, maps, shuffles and reduces.</p></div>
        <div class="rrow"><span class="n">07</span><h3>Four types</h3><p>Key-value, document, column family and graph, each with its sweet spot, usually running next to a relational database.</p></div>
      </div>
